    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAVn//xSc\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/contrib/revoke/model.py",
        "patchSetId": 79
      },
      "lineNbr": 193,
      "author": {
        "id": 2903
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-03-04T07:50:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I still feel that a lot of this logic could be moved out of \"is_revoked\" and limit the need to build the tree every time if nothing has changed.\n\nPerhaps when add_event or remove event is called a timestamp is set and the revoke event tree is only run specifically when that occurs... maybe this rebuild should occur in the syncronize call if a change has occurred.\n\nIt seems a bit heavy handed to build this every time if it can be avoided.",
      "revId": "815d5e955850d9084f484b1b8ba462d27fda776f",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAVn//sVM\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/contrib/revoke/model.py",
        "patchSetId": 79
      },
      "lineNbr": 193,
      "author": {
        "id": 2218
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-03-04T17:28:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Building the  tree *is* guarded by the syncronize call.  This code is just traversing the tree.  The \"bundle\" can be thought of like the stack, so that this is done in a single method instead of recursively.  It keeps track of position in the tree:  it is a bundle of branches, but the branches are not created here, just collected.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAVn//xSc\u003d",
      "revId": "815d5e955850d9084f484b1b8ba462d27fda776f",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
